The trap
Treating the ignore file as “set and forget”. It fails silently in the direction that matters:

a hash that a squash or rebase rewrote, or that a history rewrite removed, ignores nothing and warns about nothing;
an engineer whose clone never set blame.ignoreRevsFile sees the formatter on every line and assumes the file is broken;
a heuristic ? line can be matched to the wrong author.

And it fails loudly in the direction you notice least, until someone hits it: a global setting without :(optional) (or with it, on old Git) makes blame fatal.

Trailers
Signed-off-by: Ada Desk &lt;ada@example.com&gt;
Co-authored-by: Sam Desk &lt;sam@example.com&gt;
Fixes: #18
git interpret-trailers reads these. Signed-off-by is a project convention (Linux kernel, some forges). Use it when the project asks. Co-authored-by is how forges attribute a second author on a squash.

Label it in the message
git cherry-pick -x &lt;hash&gt;
-x appends (cherry picked from commit …) to the message. Useful on release branches so git log shows the origin hash.

Named and multiple
git stash push -m "experiment A"
git stash push -m "experiment B"
git stash list
git stash apply stash@{1}
git stash drop stash@{1}
git stash show -p stash@{0}
Numbers shift when you drop. The message is how you find them.

Case 1 — Install options
Upstream documents three paths:
# Cargo (git)
cargo install --git https://github.com/anthrofract/majjit

# One-shot Nix
nix run github:anthrofract/majjit
Flake overlay sketch (from the README):
# flake inputs / modules fragment
{
  inputs.majjit.url = "github:anthrofract/majjit";
  # ...
  nixpkgs.overlays = [ majjit.overlays.default ];
  environment.systemPackages = [ pkgs.majjit ];
}
Run Majjit from a colocated jj/Git repo (or any jj workspace). If you are still learning @ vs change id, finish that mental model first—then the TUI stops feeling like magic.
